WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.214
Welcome to Module 1 of the Azure
Security Engineering learning path:

2
00:00:04.214 --> 00:00:06.625
the baseline and the exposure register.

3
00:00:06.625 --> 00:00:09.138
A landing zone decides
what can be deployed.

4
00:00:09.138 --> 00:00:12.815
It doesn't decide what the internet
can reach, and nothing later in

5
00:00:12.815 --> 00:00:16.424
this path can be proved complete
without a list of what's exposed.

6
00:00:16.424 --> 00:00:21.198
In this module you learn how the Azure
Security Engineering implementation guide is

7
00:00:21.198 --> 00:00:25.635
put together, add the security settings
the baseline needs on top of the landing

8
00:00:25.635 --> 00:00:29.937
zone, and build the exposure register
that every later module checks against.

9
00:00:29.937 --> 00:00:34.244
It covers Steps 1 and 2 of the guide,
and takes about fifty minutes,

10
00:00:34.244 --> 00:00:37.280
plus a lab in your own test subscription.

11
00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:42.180
Why does this matter?

12
00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.617
A landing zone, as Cloud Boundary
and Landing Zone designs it,

13
00:00:45.617 --> 00:00:49.124
decides what can be deployed:
the management group hierarchy,

14
00:00:49.124 --> 00:00:53.455
the boundary policies, and the provisioning
loop every change goes through.

15
00:00:53.455 --> 00:00:56.501
What it doesn't decide is
what the internet can reach,

16
00:00:56.501 --> 00:00:58.980
what leaves the estate,
or who can read a key.

17
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.493
Those are engineered one service at a
time, and each one has to be proved.

18
00:01:03.493 --> 00:01:05.292
The proving starts with a list.

19
00:01:05.292 --> 00:01:09.467
You can't show that every public
endpoint sits behind DDoS protection

20
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:13.642
and a web application firewall if
you don't know every public endpoint.

21
00:01:13.642 --> 00:01:17.648
So this module adds the security
settings the baseline needs,

22
00:01:17.648 --> 00:01:22.320
and builds the exposure register
that Modules 2 to 5 check against.

23
00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.983
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

24
00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:32.350
You'll say which parts of Azure
security the books design and

25
00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:35.920
which the guide engineers, and
read the guide's authority labels.

26
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:39.412
You'll protect the management
group hierarchy and assign the

27
00:01:39.412 --> 00:01:43.383
security initiatives in do-not-enforce
mode at the intermediate root.

28
00:01:43.383 --> 00:01:47.620
You'll find platform resources that
don't send their logs to the workspace.

29
00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.950
You'll build the exposure register from

30
00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.633
Resource Graph and find
the rows with no owner.

31
00:01:52.633 --> 00:01:56.533
And you'll name what a G C C
tenant's commercial Azure offers,

32
00:01:56.533 --> 00:02:02.058
and the equivalent controls on AWS, Google
Cloud and Oracle Cloud Infrastructure.

33
00:02:02.058 --> 00:02:06.546
Before you start, skim Chapter 05
of Cloud Boundary and Landing Zone,

34
00:02:06.546 --> 00:02:10.633
and the guide's prerequisites:
the roles, eligible through pim.

35
00:02:10.633 --> 00:02:14.370
The lab runs in a test
subscription, never production.

36
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.145
Unit 1 is how the guide is built.

37
00:02:20.145 --> 00:02:22.382
The books already design most of this.

38
00:02:22.382 --> 00:02:26.485
Cloud Boundary and Landing Zone builds
the management group hierarchy,

39
00:02:26.485 --> 00:02:30.519
the boundary policies, the provisioning
loop, and who owns the boundary.

40
00:02:30.519 --> 00:02:35.682
Security Operations and Telemetry
chooses the posture tool on each cloud.

41
00:02:35.682 --> 00:02:40.308
Network and Transport owns the
agency's network edge and TIC.

42
00:02:40.308 --> 00:02:45.246
DNS, DHCP and IPAM owns DNS authority.

43
00:02:45.246 --> 00:02:49.595
Governance, Drift and Evidence
explains why drift is the threat.

44
00:02:49.595 --> 00:02:55.021
What none of them gives is the service-by-service
engineering of the Azure controls

45
00:02:55.021 --> 00:02:59.267
a Zero Trust assessment tests: DDoS
protection on every public I P,

46
00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:04.379
a web application firewall in prevention
mode, origins only the edge can reach,

47
00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:06.974
egress through an inspecting firewall,

48
00:03:06.974 --> 00:03:11.220
pass reachable only privately,
and keys behind role-based access.

49
00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:16.690
The guide does that, in ten steps, and links
to the books instead of restating them.

50
00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.378
Two more things from the guide's opening.

51
00:03:22.378 --> 00:03:23.690
First, labels.

52
00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:25.827
Each step carries an authority label:

53
00:03:25.827 --> 00:03:30.721
Mandated only where a federal source requires
it, Microsoft-recommended where Learn

54
00:03:30.721 --> 00:03:35.202
recommends it, and Risk-based recommended
where it's the program's own position.

55
00:03:35.202 --> 00:03:38.514
Design guidance marks the
program's choices in prose.

56
00:03:38.514 --> 00:03:43.407
And a control the tenant isn't licensed
for, or a feature a cloud doesn't offer,

57
00:03:43.407 --> 00:03:47.353
is a licensing or environment
decision, not a failed control.

58
00:03:47.353 --> 00:03:50.822
An assessment test that can't
run against it is Skipped,

59
00:03:50.822 --> 00:03:53.752
not Fail, as Zero Trust
and Assessment sets out.

60
00:03:53.752 --> 00:03:55.177
Second, order.

61
00:03:55.177 --> 00:03:57.441
Steps 1 and 2 come first.

62
00:03:57.441 --> 00:04:02.715
Steps 3 to 8 can run in parallel by
workload once the inventory exists.

63
00:04:02.715 --> 00:04:07.590
Step 9 turns each of them into policy,
and Step 10 runs from the start.

64
00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:12.122
This path follows that order:
this module is Steps 1 and 2,

65
00:04:12.122 --> 00:04:17.910
the next three modules pair Steps 3
to 8, and Module 5 is Steps 9 and 10.

66
00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:26.022
Unit 2 is the guide's Step 1, the
landing-zone security baseline.

67
00:04:26.022 --> 00:04:28.522
It adds four things on
top of the landing zone.

68
00:04:28.522 --> 00:04:32.316
First, protect the hierarchy, with
the command from Learn's page,

69
00:04:32.316 --> 00:04:37.424
Protect your resource hierarchy: creating
management groups needs authorization,

70
00:04:37.424 --> 00:04:40.197
and new subscriptions
land in a default group.

71
00:04:40.197 --> 00:04:43.875
Second, assign the Microsoft
cloud security benchmark and the

72
00:04:43.875 --> 00:04:47.835
fed-ramp initiative at the intermediate
root, in do-not-enforce mode.

73
00:04:47.835 --> 00:04:51.160
Learn's Azure Policy assignment
structure page says that

74
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.485
mode evaluates compliance
without enforcing the effect.

75
00:04:54.485 --> 00:04:59.022
Keep it there for at least one
twenty-four-hour evaluation cycle

76
00:04:59.022 --> 00:05:02.960
and one compliance review with
the I S S O; Step 9 enforces.

77
00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:07.210
Third, every hub resource sends
its logs to the workspace.

78
00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:12.260
Fourth, Defender for Cloud on every
subscription, with continuous export.

79
00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:16.585
Rollback is one command, deleting
the assignment, in minutes.

80
00:05:16.585 --> 00:05:18.784
Nothing at the root is excluded;

81
00:05:18.784 --> 00:05:22.700
exemptions come only through
Step 9, with an expiry.

82
00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:30.338
Unit 3 is Step 2, the
public exposure inventory.

83
00:05:30.338 --> 00:05:34.096
Learn's page, Secure your Azure
DDoS Protection deployment,

84
00:05:34.096 --> 00:05:38.545
recommends an inventory of every
public I P and its protection status,

85
00:05:38.545 --> 00:05:41.613
kept with Resource Graph,
and a quarterly review.

86
00:05:41.613 --> 00:05:45.377
The guide's four queries list
public I Ps with their DDoS state,

87
00:05:45.377 --> 00:05:50.321
Front Door profiles, application gateways
with their web application firewall

88
00:05:50.321 --> 00:05:54.601
policy, and pass resources whose
public network access isn't disabled.

89
00:05:54.601 --> 00:05:58.275
The first is built from Learn's
starter Resource Graph query.

90
00:05:58.275 --> 00:06:01.708
The register then adds what
the queries can't: the owner,

91
00:06:01.708 --> 00:06:05.447
the D N S names and certificates
that point at each exposure,

92
00:06:05.447 --> 00:06:08.575
and whether a web application
firewall covers it.

93
00:06:08.575 --> 00:06:09.738
Two cautions.

94
00:06:09.738 --> 00:06:13.998
The public network access query is
a starting list, not a complete one,

95
00:06:13.998 --> 00:06:17.362
because the property's name
and values vary by service.

96
00:06:17.362 --> 00:06:20.439
And the register is a map
of the attack surface:

97
00:06:20.439 --> 00:06:24.680
it stays inside the boundary,
never in a public repository.

98
00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:29.742
Where are agencies?

99
00:06:29.742 --> 00:06:32.993
The workbook's baseline is G C C Moderate.

100
00:06:32.993 --> 00:06:36.380
Microsoft 365 G C C pairs
with commercial Azure,

101
00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:39.767
so a G C C agency's
subscriptions are commercial,

102
00:06:39.767 --> 00:06:44.892
and the guide's commands run as written,
with the cloud set to Azure Cloud.

103
00:06:44.892 --> 00:06:48.322
The guide's table, Availability
in commercial Azure,

104
00:06:48.322 --> 00:06:50.954
says what to plan for, service by service.

105
00:06:50.954 --> 00:06:56.012
Front Door Standard and Premium are
available, and Front Door classic

106
00:06:56.012 --> 00:07:01.330
retires on March thirty-first, 2027,
and accepts no new profiles or domains.

107
00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:06.987
DDoS Network Protection is billed per
plan, and I P Protection per protected I P.

108
00:07:06.987 --> 00:07:10.642
Private Link uses one central
D N S zone per service.

109
00:07:10.642 --> 00:07:14.043
The FedRAMP initiatives are
built into Azure Policy.

110
00:07:14.043 --> 00:07:16.568
And the landing zone
accelerators are supported.

111
00:07:16.568 --> 00:07:20.140
Later modules name what to
plan for in their own steps.

112
00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:28.840
The guide also compares the same controls
on other clouds, because an agency's

113
00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:33.790
estate spans them, and its HR platform
may run on Oracle Cloud Infrastructure.

114
00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:39.898
For layer three and four DDoS: Azure's
infrastructure, Network or I P Protection;

115
00:07:39.898 --> 00:07:44.315
AWS Shield Standard and
Advanced; and Google Cloud Armor.

116
00:07:44.315 --> 00:07:46.027
Oracle's isn't compared.

117
00:07:46.027 --> 00:07:52.561
For a web application firewall: Azure W A
F on Front Door and Application Gateway;

118
00:07:52.561 --> 00:07:58.006
AWS W A F; Cloud Armor security
policies with the OWASP core rule set;

119
00:07:58.006 --> 00:08:03.153
and O C I W A F, as a regional policy
on a load balancer or at the edge.

120
00:08:03.153 --> 00:08:08.603
For an inspecting firewall: Azure Firewall
Premium, and O C I Network Firewall,

121
00:08:08.603 --> 00:08:13.542
built on Palo Alto Networks, with
intrusion detection and prevention,

122
00:08:13.542 --> 00:08:15.927
TLS inspection and URL filtering.

123
00:08:15.927 --> 00:08:18.793
For private access to pass: Private Link,

124
00:08:18.793 --> 00:08:22.527
Private Service Connect,
and O C I private endpoints.

125
00:08:22.527 --> 00:08:25.703
Each provider's own
documentation carries the build.

126
00:08:25.703 --> 00:08:28.914
O C I Government Cloud
availability of its W

127
00:08:28.914 --> 00:08:32.490
A F and Network Firewall
isn't yet specified.

128
00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:40.765
The lab runs the guide's own checks in
a test subscription, never production.

129
00:08:40.765 --> 00:08:46.116
Lab 1, on the module page, runs the kit on
fictional fixtures with no subscription,

130
00:08:46.116 --> 00:08:49.603
in the developer workbook,
Building Azure Security.

131
00:08:49.603 --> 00:08:52.615
This is Lab 2, and the
authors haven't run it.

132
00:08:52.615 --> 00:08:55.959
You need a test subscription
where someone has applied Step

133
00:08:55.959 --> 00:08:58.902
1 to a pilot management group,
and Reader on that group.

134
00:08:58.902 --> 00:09:01.614
Values you supply are in angle brackets.

135
00:09:01.614 --> 00:09:05.384
Step one signs in to the cloud
the subscription lives in

136
00:09:05.384 --> 00:09:08.990
and adds the two Azure C L I
extensions the guide needs.

137
00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:12.340
Step two lists the policy
assignments at the pilot group.

138
00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:14.742
The guide's Validate step says you should

139
00:09:14.742 --> 00:09:17.215
see the initiatives in
do-not-enforce mode.

140
00:09:17.215 --> 00:09:21.893
Step three lists the platform resources
that need a diagnostic setting:

141
00:09:21.893 --> 00:09:27.039
firewalls, application gateways, Front
Door profiles, key vaults and public I Ps.

142
00:09:27.039 --> 00:09:31.015
Step four takes one of them and
lists its diagnostic settings.

143
00:09:31.015 --> 00:09:34.551
Each should send its logs to
the platform team's workspace;

144
00:09:34.551 --> 00:09:37.570
one with none is a gap
for the platform owner.

145
00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:47.304
Step five makes a folder for the inventory
and runs the guide's four Step 2 queries,

146
00:09:47.304 --> 00:09:51.590
writing each to a J SON file:
public I Ps, Front Door profiles,

147
00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:55.520
application gateways, and
pass with public access on.

148
00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:57.982
The full commands are on the module page.

149
00:09:57.982 --> 00:10:01.157
Step six finds the public
I Ps with no owner.

150
00:10:01.157 --> 00:10:05.612
That filter is the path's own, not the
guide's: it reads the data array that

151
00:10:05.612 --> 00:10:10.682
the Resource Graph extension writes, and
keeps the rows whose owner tag is empty.

152
00:10:10.682 --> 00:10:14.294
The guide's Validate step says
every row should have an owner,

153
00:10:14.294 --> 00:10:16.821
or each ownerless row
should have a ticket.

154
00:10:16.821 --> 00:10:20.882
Module 5 counts these rows
again, to compute the metrics.

155
00:10:20.882 --> 00:10:22.507
Step seven backs it out.

156
00:10:22.507 --> 00:10:25.924
The lab created nothing in
Azure, but the out folder is a

157
00:10:25.924 --> 00:10:29.930
map of the test subscription's
attack surface, so delete it.

158
00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:38.735
The federal requirement, from NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three,

159
00:10:38.735 --> 00:10:42.105
revision five, as the guide's
requirements table maps it.

160
00:10:42.105 --> 00:10:47.963
Step 1 supports C M two, baseline
configuration, C M six, configuration settings,

161
00:10:47.963 --> 00:10:50.667
and C A seven, continuous monitoring,

162
00:10:50.667 --> 00:10:56.255
aligned with the fed-ramp twenty X key
security indicators the guide names.

163
00:10:56.255 --> 00:11:00.480
The evidence is the initiative
assignments and the compliance export.

164
00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:06.070
Step 2 supports C M eight, system component
inventory, and S C seven, boundary

165
00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:12.292
protection; its evidence is the dated
register, reconciled with Defender for Cloud.

166
00:11:12.292 --> 00:11:14.368
The steps support these controls.

167
00:11:14.368 --> 00:11:17.191
The organization still
sets their parameters,

168
00:11:17.191 --> 00:11:20.305
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

169
00:11:20.305 --> 00:11:24.459
The key security indicators are
written for cloud service providers;

170
00:11:24.459 --> 00:11:27.550
the agency lines its own
evidence up with them.

171
00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:33.275
Check yourself with two questions.

172
00:11:33.275 --> 00:11:38.100
First: why does Step 1 assign the security
initiatives in do-not-enforce mode?

173
00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:43.142
The answer is b: to evaluate compliance
without enforcing the effect,

174
00:11:43.142 --> 00:11:48.538
until at least one evaluation cycle and
a compliance review with the I S S O.

175
00:11:48.538 --> 00:11:49.688
Step 9 enforces.

176
00:11:49.688 --> 00:11:52.488
Second: where does the
exposure register live?

177
00:11:52.488 --> 00:11:57.650
The answer is b, inside the boundary,
because it's a map of the attack surface.

178
00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:00.063
It never goes in a public repository.

179
00:12:00.063 --> 00:12:05.340
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

180
00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:09.915
To sum up.

181
00:12:09.915 --> 00:12:14.027
You saw how the guide divides the work
with the books, and how its labels work.

182
00:12:14.027 --> 00:12:16.448
You checked the baseline's initiatives in

183
00:12:16.448 --> 00:12:19.077
audit at the pilot group,
and the platform logs.

184
00:12:19.077 --> 00:12:22.865
You built the exposure register,
with an owner on every row.

185
00:12:22.865 --> 00:12:26.539
And you saw what commercial
Azure offers a G C C tenant,

186
00:12:26.539 --> 00:12:31.715
and the same controls on AWS, Google
Cloud and Oracle Cloud Infrastructure.

187
00:12:31.715 --> 00:12:34.533
The evidence an I S S O
keeps from this module:

188
00:12:34.533 --> 00:12:38.446
the hierarchy settings and
initiative assignment exports,

189
00:12:38.446 --> 00:12:43.221
policy compliance in the workspace,
the query text and the dated register,

190
00:12:43.221 --> 00:12:46.352
and its reconciliation
with Defender for Cloud.

191
00:12:46.352 --> 00:12:51.130
Next is Module 2: DDoS protection on
every public I P in that register,

192
00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:55.720
and the web application firewall,
from detection to prevention.
